<FIELD pos="-7.4,-14.4" value="J1" description="Ref" type="0" show="1"/><FIELD pos="-7.4,-12" value="TinyCAD configurable educational equivalent" description="Simulation model" type="1" show="0"/></SYMBOL><WIRE a="50,84" b="50,79"/><LABEL pos="50,79" font="2" color="432D14" direction="3" style="0">SUPPLY</LABEL><WIRE a="50,88" b="44,88"/><LABEL pos="44,88" font="2" color="432D14" direction="3" style="0">RETURN</LABEL><WIRE a="50,92" b="50,97"/><LABEL pos="50,97" font="2" color="432D14" direction="3" style="0">TEST</LABEL><NOTE_TEXT a="7,4" b="233,16.6" font="3" color="432D14" style="3" fill="0" direction="3" border_style="0">F17 · Construction and soldering preparation</NOTE_TEXT><NOTE_TEXT a="147,21" b="233,148" font="1" color="432D14" style="4" fill="3" direction="3" border_style="0">## From a circuit to a real assembly

**BTEC First · ages 14–16**

A schematic describes electrical connections. The same connections can be arranged in many physical layouts.

### Your investigation

Trace SUPPLY through the switch, limiting resistor and LED to RETURN. Mark polarity and test points, then sketch a practical layout and have it checked.

### This activity

Produce a labelled schematic, polarity notes and a connectivity checklist for the physical assembly. TinyCAD does not turn the schematic into a breadboard photograph, demonstrate soldering competence or inspect joints.

### Getting started

This is a connection-planning drawing. Follow each labelled net, identify the real terminals and add your bench measurements. No simulation is attached.



**Explain:** What changed, and why? Use your readings as evidence.

Check the physical layout with your teacher before construction.</NOTE_TEXT></TinyCAD><DSNSETTINGS><SHOW_RULERS>1</SHOW_RULERS></DSNSETTINGS><NETLIST_TYPES><TYPE name="default"/></NETLIST_TYPES></TinyCADSheets>